<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bharuch</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Barygaza, Bharukaccha, Broach, Bhrigukaccha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bharuch is a city on the estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and one of the oldest ports of the subcontinent. Known to Greek and Roman merchants as Barygaza, it was the great harbour of western India in the early centuries CE, shipping goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and receiving wine, metals and Roman coin, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> describes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bharuch.jpg" alt="Bharuch" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: સતિષચંદ્ર · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BrSwamitemple.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
